\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Hierarchical Fourier feature construction during training. The low-rank structure, combined with frozen random features, naturally builds a hierarchical representation where lower-frequency components (coarse-scale features) are captured first, followed by progressively higher-frequency components (fine-scale details). The mixing matrix $L$ learns to combine these features efficiently, creating a well-conditioned optimization landscape that avoids exponential worst-case behavior.}}{6}{figure.caption.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:hierarchical-fourier}{{2}{6}{Hierarchical Fourier feature construction during training. The low-rank structure, combined with frozen random features, naturally builds a hierarchical representation where lower-frequency components (coarse-scale features) are captured first, followed by progressively higher-frequency components (fine-scale details). The mixing matrix $L$ learns to combine these features efficiently, creating a well-conditioned optimization landscape that avoids exponential worst-case behavior}{figure.caption.2}{}}
\newlabel{fig:hierarchical-fourier@cref}{{[figure][2][]2}{[1][6][]6}}
